k='ex-dj-bridge-row-and-column-shapes-at-rank-three'
s=r'''Let $H=H_{A,q}(S_3)$, $A=\mathbb Z[q^{\pm1}]$, and put $p_3(q)=(1+q)(1+q+q^2)$. All four modules $P((3)),P((1^3)),C_{\mathrm M}((3)),C_{\mathrm M}((1^3))$ have rank1. On $P((3))$ and $C_{\mathrm M}((3))$, both generators act by $q$; on $P((1^3))$ and $C_{\mathrm M}((1^3))$, both act by $-1$. In the canonical generators $x_{(3)},y_{(3)},c_{(3)},c_{(1^3)}$, the respective self-form values are $1,q^{-3}p_3(q),p_3(q),1$. Thus $\Phi_{(3)}(x_{(3)})=c_{(1^3)}$ has exact form multiplier $u_{(3)}=1$, whereas $\Phi_{(1^3)}(y_{(3)})=c_{(3)}$ has $u_{(1^3)}=q^3$. In both targets the action is twisted by $\theta$, so the two module identities match. At $q=1$ over $\mathbb Q$ the respective form values are $1,6,6,1$, and $\theta$ exchanges trivial and sign. These identities specialize over every commutative ring with a unit parameter. No choice principle is used.'''
st=r'''For shape $(3)$ the row permutation module has one basis vector $x_{(3)}=\sum_wT_w$ with chosen norm1, and its ordinary polytabloid generator is $x_{(3)}$; pair the six Hecke terms to get $x_{(3)}T_i=qx_{(3)}$ and $x_{(3)}^2=p_3(q)x_{(3)}$. The latter identity gives the Murphy cell norm $p_3(q)$. For shape $(1^3)$ the row module is the regular module, and $y_{(3)}=\sum_w(-q)^{-\ell(w)}T_w$ obeys $y_{(3)}T_i=-y_{(3)}$ by the same paired-term quadratic calculation. Its norm in the standard row basis is $\sum_wq^{-\ell(w)}=q^{-3}p_3(q)$, since the longest permutation has length3 and maps lengths to $3-\ell(w)$. The bottom Murphy quotient has generator1; modulo higher Murphy shapes, $1+T_i=0$, so $T_i=-1$ and its form has value1. The rank1 maps send the displayed generators as stated, and $\theta(T_i)=q-1-T_i$ exchanges the two actions. Direct comparison of norms proves both units. Evaluating $q=1$ gives the four stated integers; all identities were proved over the Laurent ring, so base change is legitimate even when $p_3(q)$ vanishes. No generic argument or quantum-factorial division is used.'''
add(k,s,st)
k='ex-dj-bridge-singular-two-tableau-shape'
s=r'''For $\lambda=(2,1)$ put $x=1+T_1$ and $b_0=x$, $b_1=xT_2$, $b_2=xT_2T_1$ in the row permutation module. Its form is $\operatorname{diag}(1,q,q^2)$ and its generator action matrices, with coordinate row vectors, are $R_1=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}q&0&0\\0&0&1\\0&q&q-1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, $R_2=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&0\\q&q-1&0\\0&0&q\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. The ordinary polytabloid basis is $p_0=z=xT_2(1-q^{-1}T_1)=b_1-q^{-1}b_2$, $p_1=zT_2=qb_0+(q-1)b_1-b_2$. Their embedding has unit determinant on its $b_0,b_2$ coordinates, and their form is
$$B=\begin{pmatrix}q+1&q^2\\q^2&q^3+q\end{pmatrix}.$$
In the Murphy quotient let $c_0=x$, $c_1=xT_2$ modulo the higher shape $(3)$. Its form and generator action matrices, with coordinate row vectors, are
$$G=\begin{pmatrix}q+1&-1\\-1&q^2+1\end{pmatrix},\quad M_1=\begin{pmatrix}q&0\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},\quad M_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\q&q-1\end{pmatrix}.$$
On the ordinary basis the matrices are $P_1=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}-1&0\\-q^2&q\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and $P_2=M_2$. The map $\Phi(p_0)=c_0$, $\Phi(p_1)=(q-1)c_0-c_1$ has matrix $A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\q-1&-1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$; it satisfies $P_iA=A((q-1)I-M_i)$ and $AGA^{\mathsf T}=B$. The same-shape perfect cross-pairing on the bases $(c_0,c_1)$ and $(p_0,p_1)$ has matrix $Q=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&q\\q&q(q-1)\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ with determinant $-q^2$, a unit; its entries are the row-form coefficients before quotienting. Thus the exact self-form multiplier is1. Both Gram determinants equal $q(1+q+q^2)$. At $q=-1$ over characteristic zero these forms are nonsingular with determinant $-1$, even though $[2]_q=0$; at $q=1$ in characteristic2 both reduce to $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ and have radical0. This distinguishes a singular algebra parameter from degeneracy of this particular module form. No choice principle is used.'''
st=r'''The row module action follows by the Hecke quadratic and braid relations: $b_0T_1=qb_0$, $b_1T_1=b_2$, $b_2T_1=qb_1+(q-1)b_2$; $b_0T_2=b_1$, $b_1T_2=qb_0+(q-1)b_1$, $b_2T_2=qb_2$. Substituting the displayed $p_0,p_1$ gives $p_0T_1=-p_0$, $p_1T_1=-q^2p_0+qp_1$, $p_0T_2=p_1$, $p_1T_2=qp_0+(q-1)p_1$. The projection of $p_0,p_1$ onto coordinates $b_0,b_2$ has matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&-q^{-1}\\q&-1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ with determinant1, proving a split free rank2 submodule over every ring. It contains the generator and is stable under both Hecke generators, so it is exactly $zH$, without using a general polytabloid-basis theorem. In the Murphy cell quotient the higher vector $x_{(3)}=b_0+b_1+b_2$ vanishes, so $b_2=-c_0-c_1$; the preceding action gives $M_i$. Cell multiplication gives $G_{00}=q+1$, $G_{01}=-1$, $G_{11}=q(q+1)-(q-1)=q^2+1$. Computing the row-basis weighted inner products gives $B$. Direct matrix multiplication verifies both intertwining identities and $AGA^{\mathsf T}=B$; $\det A=-1$ is a unit. Evaluating the two Gram matrices gives the stated radicals. Exact integer-polynomial verification is recorded separately in rank-three-symbolic-check.json; that finite check supports these calculations and is not a proof of the general comparison theorem.'''
add(k,s,st)
k='cex-dj-bridge-untwisted-conjugation-changes-the-module'
s=r'''Over $\mathbb Q$ at $q=1$ and $n=3$, the Murphy cell module $C_{\mathrm M}((1^3))$ is sign with $T_i=-1$, while the ordinary polytabloid module $P((3))$ and its contragredient dual are trivial with $T_i=1$. Hence $C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda')\cong P(\lambda)^\circ$ without $\theta$ is false in general. The correct bridge is $C_{\mathrm M}(\lambda')\cong P(\lambda)^\theta$; combining it with same-shape duality gives $P(\lambda')\cong P(\lambda)^{\circ,\theta}$, which is a different formula. This reproduces the source20 countermodel from the standalone new foundations. No choice principle is used.'''
st=r'''The top row symmetrizer $x_{(3)}=\sum_{w\in S_3}w$ spans $P((3))$ and is fixed by every permutation, so this module and its dual are trivial. In the one-dimensional bottom Murphy cell, the higher elements $1+s_1$ and $1+s_2$ vanish, so each $s_i=-1$. A module isomorphism from the bottom cell to the trivial module would intertwine these scalar operators; over $\mathbb Q$, $-1\ne1$, so no such nonzero isomorphism exists. The automorphism $\theta$ sends $s_i$ to $-s_i$ and therefore corrects the action. The general corrected formulas follow by the two separately proved bridge theorems, not by treating the ordinary Specht module as always self-dual. This finite counterexample uses no Schur theory or old convention definition.'''
